WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:05.640 --> 00:00:07.320
Walk downtown in Seattle

00:00:07.320 --> 00:00:09.283
and you're likely to come across this.

00:00:10.650 --> 00:00:12.603
Oh, sorry. I mean this.

00:00:14.190 --> 00:00:16.780
It's technically an Amazon office

00:00:16.780 --> 00:00:19.490
but really it's a gaudy show piece.

00:00:19.490 --> 00:00:21.730
Look at us. We're Amazon.

00:00:21.730 --> 00:00:25.570
We built a rainforest smack
dab in the middle of Seattle

00:00:25.570 --> 00:00:29.390
and encased it in silver
balls because we can.

00:00:29.390 --> 00:00:32.190
It's the kind of thing
that seems like a good idea

00:00:32.190 --> 00:00:34.790
right after you've drained your blood boy

00:00:34.790 --> 00:00:37.110
but that you later regret.

00:00:37.110 --> 00:00:39.440
Next thing you know, you
have to build a stand

00:00:39.440 --> 00:00:41.550
right by your giant balls

00:00:41.550 --> 00:00:44.543
and give away free fruit
all day as an apology.

00:00:45.850 --> 00:00:47.700
We're sorry for what we've done,

00:00:47.700 --> 00:00:49.633
have as many bananas as you'd like.

00:01:05.880 --> 00:01:07.240
Seattle's obscene wealth

00:01:07.240 --> 00:01:09.450
has turned the real estate market here

00:01:09.450 --> 00:01:11.610
into a brutal free for all.

00:01:11.610 --> 00:01:13.900
Just ask these people.

00:01:13.900 --> 00:01:17.070
The sprint to find permanent housing here,

00:01:17.070 --> 00:01:20.820
whether you're buying
or renting is insane.

00:01:20.820 --> 00:01:22.360
And to actually buy a place

00:01:23.230 --> 00:01:25.040
feels out of the realm of possibility

00:01:25.040 --> 00:01:26.340
unless you're a millionaire.

00:01:26.340 --> 00:01:28.660
It just seems like it keeps
getting worse and worse.

00:01:28.660 --> 00:01:31.440
The inventory is pretty tight.

00:01:31.440 --> 00:01:33.500
It's hard to find really good stuff.

00:01:33.500 --> 00:01:34.680
I think renting was easier.

00:01:34.680 --> 00:01:36.780
I was kind of shocked at
how expensive everything is

00:01:36.780 --> 00:01:39.350
and how competitive the market is.

00:01:39.350 --> 00:01:44.350
The Seattle housing scene
in one word -- crazy.

00:01:44.380 --> 00:01:45.270
I'd say it's a gauntlet.

00:01:45.270 --> 00:01:46.190
It's cutthroat.

00:01:46.190 --> 00:01:47.553
Mm. Trouble.

00:01:49.620 --> 00:01:52.110
To learn about the latest
in Seattle real estate

00:01:52.110 --> 00:01:54.370
and the art of selling a home here,

00:01:54.370 --> 00:01:56.490
I went straight to the top.

00:01:56.490 --> 00:01:57.323
Let's roll.

00:01:59.060 --> 00:02:02.350
I paid a visit to my old
friend, Glenn Kelman,

00:02:02.350 --> 00:02:04.570
the CEO of Redfin.

00:02:04.570 --> 00:02:08.360
I should warn you that almost no one

00:02:08.360 --> 00:02:10.730
is willing to get in the car
with me while I'm driving.

00:02:10.730 --> 00:02:12.917
It's too late now.

00:02:12.917 --> 00:02:15.140
And if at any point you
need to grab the wheel

00:02:15.140 --> 00:02:17.210
to save us from death, I won't resist.

00:02:17.210 --> 00:02:18.390
How do you feel about the yoke?

00:02:18.390 --> 00:02:19.670
It's terrible.
Yeah.

00:02:19.670 --> 00:02:24.580
It's Elon Musk just being
opinionated to be opinionated

00:02:24.580 --> 00:02:26.220
without actually being better.

00:02:26.220 --> 00:02:28.360
I have this theory, by the way,

00:02:28.360 --> 00:02:32.470
I think about when people can
stand up to CEOs all the time,

00:02:32.470 --> 00:02:35.210
'cause I worry that people
can't stand up to me.

00:02:35.210 --> 00:02:39.720
And for me, the signal version
of that is Jar Jar Binks

00:02:39.720 --> 00:02:43.520
where clearly, no one
can tell George Lucas

00:02:43.520 --> 00:02:48.250
what a racist disaster Jar Jar
was because he was surrounded

00:02:48.250 --> 00:02:52.580
by 100, like 18 layers of sycophants.

00:02:52.580 --> 00:02:54.990
I think no one could tell Elon

00:02:54.990 --> 00:02:58.630
this is an accident waiting to
happen, this steering wheel.

00:02:58.630 --> 00:03:02.420
And I can't figure out what
mistakes Redfin is making

00:03:02.420 --> 00:03:04.873
that no one has the
courage to tell me about.

00:03:07.320 --> 00:03:09.878
I used Redfin back in the, yeah.

00:03:09.878 --> 00:03:11.136
I didn't know that. Really?

00:03:11.136 --> 00:03:12.756
Yeah, yeah. I told, you did know it.

00:03:12.756 --> 00:03:13.621
No.
Once. You did.

00:03:13.621 --> 00:03:15.280
I store that shit.

00:03:15.280 --> 00:03:17.070
I shouldn't have said the S word, but--

00:03:17.070 --> 00:03:18.660
No, you can.

00:03:18.660 --> 00:03:20.467
No, you did, 'cause I
had gotten to know you

00:03:20.467 --> 00:03:21.650
and I was like, oh fine,

00:03:21.650 --> 00:03:23.620
I'll give this Redfin thing a try, then.

00:03:23.620 --> 00:03:25.260
I feel like you've dated me

00:03:25.260 --> 00:03:27.390
when I was a teenager and had braces.

00:03:27.390 --> 00:03:28.763
It's so much better now.

00:03:30.900 --> 00:03:35.420
So technology is supposed to
make industries more efficient.

00:03:35.420 --> 00:03:37.760
With real estate, we just
found the perfect foil

00:03:37.760 --> 00:03:39.850
where you have an agent

00:03:40.760 --> 00:03:44.840
who isn't always acting in
the customer's best interest

00:03:44.840 --> 00:03:46.630
but also charging a high price.

00:03:46.630 --> 00:03:49.870
And so we thought that by using technology

00:03:49.870 --> 00:03:51.820
we could make the agent more efficient.

00:03:53.330 --> 00:03:54.710
We're on our way to see a home

00:03:54.710 --> 00:03:59.140
that Redfin bought, refurbished
and then sold for a profit.

00:03:59.140 --> 00:04:00.830
Buying and flipping homes like this

00:04:00.830 --> 00:04:03.280
is a huge risk for the company.

00:04:03.280 --> 00:04:05.270
Just ask Zillow.

00:04:05.270 --> 00:04:07.100
If housing prices go down,

00:04:07.100 --> 00:04:10.710
Redfin might get stuck with
money losing inventory.

00:04:10.710 --> 00:04:13.640
Glenn's belief is that
Redfin can use its technology

00:04:13.640 --> 00:04:16.100
and experience to lower the risks.

00:04:16.100 --> 00:04:18.190
It analyzes thousands of data points

00:04:18.190 --> 00:04:19.930
to monitor quick changing trends

00:04:19.930 --> 00:04:22.170
in housing markets across the country.

00:04:22.170 --> 00:04:25.520
A mix of machine learning
algorithms and computer vision

00:04:25.520 --> 00:04:27.470
tell Redfin where to buy homes

00:04:27.470 --> 00:04:30.823
and what quick fixes will
result in the best returns.

00:04:33.270 --> 00:04:36.510
First of all, there's just
the fundamental problem

00:04:36.510 --> 00:04:38.970
of trying to figure out
what a home is worth.

00:04:38.970 --> 00:04:42.680
And the reason that you need
machine learning algorithms

00:04:42.680 --> 00:04:44.690
rather than standard computer science

00:04:44.690 --> 00:04:48.460
is because a basement is
very valuable in Seattle,

00:04:48.460 --> 00:04:51.060
whereas a pool is valuable in Atlanta.

00:04:51.060 --> 00:04:56.060
It is constantly reevaluating
what factors matter the most.

00:04:56.730 --> 00:04:59.780
And so one of them is a pricing algorithm.

00:04:59.780 --> 00:05:04.460
And then another one is about
renovations in computer vision

00:05:04.460 --> 00:05:06.650
and trying to assess the
quality of the property

00:05:06.650 --> 00:05:07.760
based on photos.

00:05:07.760 --> 00:05:08.760
Oh, okay.

00:05:11.890 --> 00:05:14.830
The little house here
used to look like this,

00:05:14.830 --> 00:05:18.330
cozy, but not exactly Seattle's finest.

00:05:18.330 --> 00:05:21.263
Unless you're into
smoke-tinged yellowing walls.

00:05:22.230 --> 00:05:23.850
Redfin has revamped the place

00:05:23.850 --> 00:05:26.720
in the hopes of kicking its price higher.

00:05:26.720 --> 00:05:30.100
And Heather here is one of
Redfin's chief revampers.

00:05:30.100 --> 00:05:32.960
This isn't necessarily someone's
individual personality.

00:05:32.960 --> 00:05:35.290
And you know, we're just
trying to position the house

00:05:35.290 --> 00:05:38.033
so people can see what the potential is.

00:05:43.130 --> 00:05:45.404
So we wanna keep it kind of neutral.

00:05:45.404 --> 00:05:47.471
Are there any other just tiny details

00:05:47.471 --> 00:05:49.531
that people wouldn't think of,
like the kind of faucet or--

00:05:49.531 --> 00:05:51.340
I think anything that you touch, right?

00:05:51.340 --> 00:05:54.460
So that's why we wanna put
in new plumbing fixtures

00:05:54.460 --> 00:05:57.360
or we wanna make sure
that there's new knobs

00:05:57.360 --> 00:06:00.480
because I think that makes it feel

00:06:00.480 --> 00:06:01.850
like it's a good experience.

00:06:01.850 --> 00:06:03.530
Okay. Yeah, no, that makes sense.

00:06:03.530 --> 00:06:06.510
The simplest thing that
has the highest ROI

00:06:06.510 --> 00:06:09.783
is just update the kitchen
without replacing it.

00:06:12.070 --> 00:06:13.620
Paint is just transformative,

00:06:13.620 --> 00:06:17.160
so paint your whole house
and keep it neutral.

00:06:17.160 --> 00:06:18.080
Makes sense.

00:06:18.080 --> 00:06:21.250
Make sure your flooring is consistent.

00:06:21.250 --> 00:06:24.310
This might seem like
normal renovation stuff,

00:06:24.310 --> 00:06:26.983
but it's all pulled from
Redfin's algorithms.

00:06:27.880 --> 00:06:29.900
We're doing 5,000 sales a year

00:06:29.900 --> 00:06:32.160
and we're capturing data
about what works and doesn't

00:06:32.160 --> 00:06:35.610
and the simplest lesson
is to not overdo it.

00:06:35.610 --> 00:06:36.890
When we have lost our shirt,

00:06:36.890 --> 00:06:40.500
it's almost always doing
too much, not too little.

00:06:40.500 --> 00:06:43.270
How many times did you
screw it up before you guys,

00:06:43.270 --> 00:06:44.103
you know, is this--

00:06:44.103 --> 00:06:45.993
You ask as if we stopped screwing it up.

00:06:46.920 --> 00:06:49.150
The promise of Redfin has long centered

00:06:49.150 --> 00:06:50.670
on lowering commission rates

00:06:50.670 --> 00:06:52.540
and trying to modernize real estate

00:06:52.540 --> 00:06:55.470
and some of its dubious practices.

00:06:55.470 --> 00:06:58.600
Redfin, however, has
faced recent criticism

00:06:58.600 --> 00:07:01.210
and even been sued for
offering better services

00:07:01.210 --> 00:07:02.703
to affluent customers.

00:07:03.540 --> 00:07:06.040
Glenn says he takes
these critiques to heart

00:07:06.040 --> 00:07:08.660
and he promises that he's
more than some rich guy

00:07:08.660 --> 00:07:11.253
capitalizing off a crazed
real estate market.

00:07:12.110 --> 00:07:15.420
People pay 60 to $80 billion
in real estate commissions.

00:07:15.420 --> 00:07:17.330
And no one would ever pay that

00:07:17.330 --> 00:07:19.120
if you actually had to dig out your wallet

00:07:19.120 --> 00:07:21.930
and hand a real estate
agent 30 or $40,000.

00:07:21.930 --> 00:07:26.930
So technology is supposed to
make industries more efficient.

00:07:27.150 --> 00:07:29.930
The second part of it is
more about social justice

00:07:29.930 --> 00:07:34.720
that still lenders who
decide that someone who is

00:07:34.720 --> 00:07:37.410
Latino or Black is less likely
to be able to afford a home

00:07:37.410 --> 00:07:39.930
and even if they got the same income.

00:07:39.930 --> 00:07:43.900
Why would your agents be less inclined

00:07:43.900 --> 00:07:48.010
to do those sorts of things
than others historically?

00:07:48.010 --> 00:07:51.950
You can't control any
human being in every way,

00:07:51.950 --> 00:07:54.510
but we can certainly say
that when you list a home,

00:07:54.510 --> 00:07:56.210
you need to make sure
that everybody can see

00:07:56.210 --> 00:07:59.293
that it's for sale instead of
just posting it on Facebook,

00:08:00.160 --> 00:08:02.760
which has become especially
popular in Silicon Valley.

00:08:02.760 --> 00:08:04.800
You need to give people
fair housing training.

00:08:04.800 --> 00:08:07.910
So I'm not trying to get on
my high horse on this one

00:08:07.910 --> 00:08:12.060
and say that we're the end all
and be all of fair housing.

00:08:12.060 --> 00:08:14.370
But the way that housing has been working

00:08:14.370 --> 00:08:15.540
over the past 50 years

00:08:15.540 --> 00:08:18.173
can't be the way it
works over the next 50.

00:08:19.910 --> 00:08:22.800
Okay, so you've been in
Seattle now for a while

00:08:22.800 --> 00:08:26.240
and I know you don't wanna
bad mouth the home team,

00:08:26.240 --> 00:08:27.887
but you were--
I love Seattle but--

00:08:27.887 --> 00:08:30.060
But you felt like Vancouver's

00:08:30.060 --> 00:08:32.453
kind of where the action is or?

00:08:33.830 --> 00:08:36.430
Yeah well, it's almost
a tale of two cities

00:08:36.430 --> 00:08:39.970
because first of all,

00:08:39.970 --> 00:08:42.510
Vancouver just has different
zoning than Seattle.

00:08:42.510 --> 00:08:43.900
So when you go there, you'll see,

00:08:43.900 --> 00:08:46.490
it sometimes feels like Hong Kong.

00:08:46.490 --> 00:08:48.580
It's very dense and bustling

00:08:48.580 --> 00:08:51.193
and Seattle has begun to address that.

00:08:52.120 --> 00:08:54.970
But for a long time, what really
shaped the Seattle skyline

00:08:54.970 --> 00:08:58.230
was this rumor among developers

00:08:58.230 --> 00:09:01.610
that Jeff Bezos didn't like buildings

00:09:01.610 --> 00:09:04.790
that were taller than eight or 12 stories.

00:09:04.790 --> 00:09:08.190
The windows had to open and
it had to be tall enough

00:09:08.190 --> 00:09:10.470
or low enough that you
could take the stairs.

00:09:10.470 --> 00:09:13.540
And now the second part is that Canada

00:09:13.540 --> 00:09:16.130
is outranking the United
States as a destination

00:09:16.130 --> 00:09:18.230
for international tech talent.

00:09:18.230 --> 00:09:20.770
You have all these foreign students

00:09:20.770 --> 00:09:22.520
who believe in the Canadian dream

00:09:22.520 --> 00:09:23.930
instead of the American dream

00:09:23.930 --> 00:09:28.930
and I think it's really
becoming this tech center.

00:09:29.050 --> 00:09:30.100
Some of it's urban planning

00:09:30.100 --> 00:09:31.520
and some of it's immigration policy.

00:09:31.520 --> 00:09:35.580
Yeah. But you're hanging tight in Seattle.

00:09:35.580 --> 00:09:37.360
When you actually think

00:09:37.360 --> 00:09:39.700
about turning in your American passport

00:09:40.680 --> 00:09:45.173
and becoming Canadian and
pumping your gas in liters,

00:09:47.180 --> 00:09:48.460
it's still a step.

00:09:48.460 --> 00:09:49.293
Yeah.

00:09:58.320 --> 00:10:00.410
No trip to Seattle is really complete

00:10:00.410 --> 00:10:02.063
without some time on the water.

00:10:02.910 --> 00:10:06.490
If you're lucky enough, you
can find a nerdy sea captain

00:10:06.490 --> 00:10:08.403
who will take you out with his crew.

00:10:15.930 --> 00:10:18.480
Nathan here used to work
with the other Nathan

00:10:18.480 --> 00:10:20.350
at Intellectual Ventures.

00:10:20.350 --> 00:10:22.680
Then he started a company called Kymeta

00:10:22.680 --> 00:10:24.860
that made crazy antennas.

00:10:24.860 --> 00:10:29.080
And now he started another
company named Rendered.ai.

00:10:29.080 --> 00:10:32.800
It makes something called synthetic data.

00:10:32.800 --> 00:10:36.610
How are you making the first
image of this fake x-ray?

00:10:36.610 --> 00:10:37.443
I mean, this--

00:10:37.443 --> 00:10:38.970
Literally you can build 3D models,

00:10:38.970 --> 00:10:42.114
in Blender or other tools and
then simulate them using that.

00:10:42.114 --> 00:10:44.300
And this would just either
make many, many, many

00:10:44.300 --> 00:10:46.160
x-ray images that then get fed

00:10:46.160 --> 00:10:48.810
into a machine learning system
to see what it can spot?

00:10:48.810 --> 00:10:51.080
You go through the airport, right?

00:10:51.080 --> 00:10:52.840
They of course scan your baggage

00:10:52.840 --> 00:10:55.243
and that's going through an AI.

00:10:55.243 --> 00:10:56.880
Do you know how they train that AI?

00:10:56.880 --> 00:10:58.780
They literally have people pack bags

00:10:58.780 --> 00:10:59.810
and pass them through that

00:10:59.810 --> 00:11:03.810
so they can be like, that's
a knife, that's a banana.

00:11:03.810 --> 00:11:06.160
That is done by hand today.
Okay.

00:11:06.160 --> 00:11:07.760
So as you can see that has some gaps.

00:11:07.760 --> 00:11:10.280
They just have people
spending thousands of hours

00:11:10.280 --> 00:11:12.328
passing things through?

00:11:12.328 --> 00:11:13.990
They do.
Okay.

00:11:13.990 --> 00:11:15.410
Modern machine learning AI

00:11:15.410 --> 00:11:18.020
needs to go through a
process called training

00:11:18.020 --> 00:11:19.610
where it's fed tons of data

00:11:19.610 --> 00:11:22.130
in order to learn how to do a task.

00:11:22.130 --> 00:11:24.560
Redfin, for example, feeds its algorithms

00:11:24.560 --> 00:11:26.400
data about real estate.

00:11:26.400 --> 00:11:28.640
But unlike Redfin, not every company

00:11:28.640 --> 00:11:30.940
has tons of information at its disposal

00:11:30.940 --> 00:11:33.460
to train its machine learning algorithms.

00:11:33.460 --> 00:11:36.700
So what rendered AI
does is manufacture data

00:11:36.700 --> 00:11:38.100
out of thin air.

00:11:38.100 --> 00:11:41.210
It then feeds AI algorithms
with this information

00:11:41.210 --> 00:11:43.963
to teach them how to
perform certain tasks.

00:11:45.700 --> 00:11:49.890
These are all synthetically created bags.

00:11:49.890 --> 00:11:54.870
This is like add a phone
and add a lipstick.

00:11:54.870 --> 00:11:56.922
You don't just simulate the
x-ray simulation, right?

00:11:56.922 --> 00:11:58.130
You can simulate the packing itself,

00:11:58.130 --> 00:12:00.080
you can simulate the folding of clothes.

00:12:00.080 --> 00:12:02.990
So we have a lot of different
simulations on the platform.

00:12:02.990 --> 00:12:06.510
So we do satellite imagery,
we've done infrared imagery,

00:12:06.510 --> 00:12:08.380
we've done security cameras.

00:12:08.380 --> 00:12:10.060
We've actually done microscopy

00:12:10.060 --> 00:12:12.200
and studied oo sites
in human reproduction.

00:12:12.200 --> 00:12:14.060
So you're making fake cells?

00:12:14.060 --> 00:12:16.263
Well, yeah, like fake embryos, literally.

00:12:17.160 --> 00:12:19.780
Sometimes you wanna run
tests to see if the AI

00:12:19.780 --> 00:12:22.913
behind an expensive piece of
technology will even work.

00:12:25.880 --> 00:12:28.610
For example, startups are
rushing to send satellites

00:12:28.610 --> 00:12:31.883
into orbit that photograph
the earth in different ways.

00:12:32.740 --> 00:12:34.410
They're packed full of expensive,

00:12:34.410 --> 00:12:36.393
often experimental sensors.

00:12:37.440 --> 00:12:40.350
Rendered AI can help gauge if
these sensors will do the job

00:12:40.350 --> 00:12:42.360
they're supposed to before you pay

00:12:42.360 --> 00:12:44.543
to build and launch the satellites.

00:12:45.500 --> 00:12:46.780
Well, you could imagine questions like,

00:12:46.780 --> 00:12:49.010
hey, I wanna detect forest fires, right?

00:12:49.010 --> 00:12:52.490
Okay. I've got visible cameras in space.

00:12:52.490 --> 00:12:55.310
How much sooner can I detect a forest fire

00:12:55.310 --> 00:12:57.220
if I was detecting an AI?

00:12:57.220 --> 00:12:59.410
So you guys could sort of simulate

00:12:59.410 --> 00:13:01.390
what forest fires would
look like from space

00:13:01.390 --> 00:13:02.900
and do that millions of times

00:13:02.900 --> 00:13:05.960
and then maybe we start
to see that these patterns

00:13:05.960 --> 00:13:07.910
kind of emerge before one breaks out.

00:13:07.910 --> 00:13:08.743
Exactly.

00:13:10.110 --> 00:13:12.880
What Nathan is talking
about is a weird concept.

00:13:12.880 --> 00:13:16.270
The AIs need information to
become good at their job.

00:13:16.270 --> 00:13:20.030
And they're now being
fed by other AI systems.

00:13:20.030 --> 00:13:24.567
Soon he imagines AI's training
the AIs that train the AIs.

00:13:26.080 --> 00:13:27.580
What we're doing with synthetic data

00:13:27.580 --> 00:13:30.730
is about not just training the AI,

00:13:30.730 --> 00:13:32.610
but training the tools that allow us

00:13:32.610 --> 00:13:35.730
to do this world building
and procedural generation

00:13:35.730 --> 00:13:37.370
and it does feed on itself.

00:13:37.370 --> 00:13:39.500
And absolutely, that's
gonna lead to all sorts

00:13:39.500 --> 00:13:42.140
of crazy and unexpected things.

00:13:42.140 --> 00:13:43.880
Although I will say,

00:13:43.880 --> 00:13:47.310
I'd start by being able to
successfully detect cars.

00:13:47.310 --> 00:13:48.307
Yeah.

00:13:48.307 --> 00:13:49.520
It'll be really good

00:13:49.520 --> 00:13:51.053
if we can do that really well.

00:13:56.020 --> 00:13:57.220
Everybody ready to tack?

00:13:58.100 --> 00:13:59.300
All right. Coming about.

00:14:08.673 --> 00:14:10.950
Perhaps there's a divine entity up there

00:14:10.950 --> 00:14:13.823
with a giant computer
simulating all of this.

00:14:14.690 --> 00:14:18.370
If so, well done Supreme Being.

00:14:18.370 --> 00:14:21.600
But for the moment, I'm
gonna assume this is real

00:14:21.600 --> 00:14:23.303
and I'm going to revel in it.

00:14:27.180 --> 00:14:29.000
Next up on "Hello World,"

00:14:29.000 --> 00:14:32.043
I head to Canada to
pound some maple syrup.

00:14:35.150 --> 00:14:36.420
It's pure.

00:14:36.420 --> 00:14:39.473
And visit a not so paranoid Android.

